sub log2 {
  my $x = shift;
  $x = log($x) / log(2);
  $x = int($x + 0.5);
  return $x;
}
sub my_die {
  my $text = shift;
  print STDERR "Fatal Error: ";
  print STDERR $text;
  print STDERR "\n";
  exit(1);
}
sub my_warn {
  my $text = shift;
  print STDERR "Warning: ";
  print STDERR $text;
  print STDERR "\n";
}
sub my_info {
  my $text = shift;
#  print STDERR "Information: ";
  print STDERR "   ";
  print STDERR $text;
  print STDERR "\n";
}
sub my_help {
  print "
$0: Auto memory generator. Instantiate the required rams for the ARM926EJS.

Usage: $0 [-h] [source top level] [destination top level] [ram-config]

The [source top level] is processed, replacing the rams with those
defined in ram-config. It relies on hooks in the [source top level] in
order to know where to place the rams. The results are placed in
[destination top level]. If no [destination top level] is given
[source top level] is used for the output.

There are two configuration files. \"memory-config\" defines the core
interface to the memories. This would not normally be modified unless
extra logic is to be inserted between the core and the memories.
\"ram-config\" is the main configuration file which defines a set of
rams, lists which rams to be used for each memory and also the size of
the instruction and data caches.

A .vh and a .vc file are also written out to the same file as
[destination top level] with a \"h\" and \"c\" appended.

After the top level and the .vh file have been written out you will
probably need to link then to ARM926EJS.v and ARM926EJS.vh in the
MAIN_ARM926EJS directory.

";
}
